What is the use of source command?
The source command (or simply .) executes a script within the current shell environment. This is specifically useful when the script defines environment variables or functions that need to persist and be accessible in the shell session that launched it.
How can you debug a shell script?
The two most common methods are:
Execution Trace: Running the script with bash -x script.sh displays every command after parameter substitution and before execution.
Syntax Check: Running the script with bash -n script.sh checks the script for syntax errors without actually executing any of the commands.
